              "name": "The Eight Major Branches of Chemistry",
              "desc": "The field of chemistry is very vast. To cover all the areas of chemistry, it is divided into different branches:\n\n1. Physical Chemistry: Concerned with the relationship between the physical properties of substances along with the chemical changes in them.\n\n2. Organic Chemistry: Study of those compounds which contains Carbon and hydrogen called hydrocarbons and their derivatives.\n\n3. Inorganic Chemistry: Deals with the study of all the elements and compounds, except hydrocarbons and their derivatives.\n\n4. Analytical Chemistry: Deals with the study of qualitative (type / kind) and quantitative (amount / nature) analysis of matter.\n\n5. Industrial Chemistry: Deals with the study of techniques and chemical processes for the preparation of different industrial products e.g. cement, glass, fertilizers etc.\n\n6. Nuclear Chemistry: Concerned with the study of nucleus, changes occurring in the nucleus, properties of the particles present in the nucleus and the emission or absorption of radiation from the nucleus.\n\n7. Biochemistry: Concerned with the study of synthesis, composition, decomposition and chemical reaction of substances, which take place in the living organisms.\n\n8. Environmental Chemistry: Concerned with the interaction of chemical substances/processes with the environment and their impact on it.\n\n--- Test Yourself (Page 5) ---\n(a) A chemist mostly uses which branch of chemistry to determine the amount of an impurity in drinking water?\nAnswer: Analytical Chemistry (Quantitative analysis).\n\n(b) A chemist in which branch of chemistry determines the composition of substance and determine their masses?\nAnswer: Analytical Chemistry.\n\n(c) A chemist develops a medicine to help patients with thyroid problems. In which branch of chemistry is this chemist working?\nAnswer: Biochemistry / Medicinal Chemistry / Organic Chemistry.",
