              "num": "1.3.2",
              "name": "Relative Atomic Mass, a.m.u. & Average Atomic Mass",
              "desc": "1.2.3 Relative Atomic Mass and Atomic Mass Unit:\nIf we measure the mass of an atom in grams or kilograms, it will be an extremely small value (10^-24 to 10^-22 g). Measuring such a small masses are not only impossible but are also impractical to work with. For that purpose, atomic masses are expressed by comparing with a mass of standard atom. The atom chosen nowadays, as the standard for comparison, is that of carbon-12, which has a mass of exactly 12 units.\n\nMasses of atoms are expressed relative to the mass of a Carbon-12 atom (1 atom of C-12 = 12 units, 1/12 the mass of C-12 = 1 unit).\nThe relative atomic mass of an element is the mass of an atom of that element relative to the mass of 1/12th of C-12.\n\n(i) Atomic Mass Unit (a.m.u.):\nAtomic mass unit is the mass of the 1/12th of an atom of C-12 (1 amu = 1.67 × 10^-24 g = 1.67 × 10^-27 kg).\n\nTable 1.6: Some Elements with their Relative Atomic Masses (Page 19):\n1. Hydrogen → 1.0079 a.m.u.\n2. Oxygen → 15.9994 a.m.u.\n3. Sodium → 22.9897 a.m.u.\n4. Magnesium → 24.3050 a.m.u.\n5. Silver → 107.8682 a.m.u.\n\n(ii) Average Atomic Mass:\nThe atomic masses are rarely found to be exactly whole numbers because most elements are composed of two or more naturally occurring isotopes and the relative atomic mass takes into account the abundance of each isotope.\n\nAverage atomic mass is the weighted average of the atomic masses of the naturally occurring isotopes of an element:\nAverage atomic mass = [Atomic mass of 1st Isotope × % abundance / 100] + [Atomic mass of 2nd Isotope × % abundance / 100]\n\nExample Calculation for Chlorine:\nChlorine has two isotopes: 35Cl (75% abundance) and 37Cl (25% abundance).\nAverage atomic mass = (35 × 75 / 100) + (37 × 25 / 100) = 26.25 + 9.25 = 35.50 a.m.u.\n\nPractice Problem 1.2 (Page 20):\n• Gallium consists of 60.108% 69Ga (mass 68.9256 amu) and 39.892% 71Ga (mass 70.9247 amu). Calculate average atomic mass:\nAverage atomic mass = (68.9256 × 60.108 / 100) + (70.9247 × 39.892 / 100) = 41.4298 + 28.2933 = 69.723 amu.\n• Neon consists of 90.51% 20Ne (19.992 amu), 0.27% 21Ne (20.994 amu), and 9.22% 22Ne (21.991 amu):\nAverage atomic mass = (19.992 × 0.9051) + (20.994 × 0.0027) + (21.991 × 0.0922) = 18.0948 + 0.0567 + 2.0276 = 20.18 amu.",
              "sciNote": "Carbon-12 was adopted as the universal atomic mass standard by IUPAC in 1961, replacing earlier Oxygen-16 and Hydrogen-1 scales.",
              "sq": "Why is the relative atomic mass of Chlorine printed as 35.5 amu rather than a whole integer?",
