              "num": "1.4.2",
              "name": "Molecules, Atomicity & Classification (Table 1.9)",
              "desc": "1.3.2 Molecule:\nA molecule is mostly formed by the chemical combination of atoms. It is the smallest unit of a substance. Molecule is the smallest particle of matter which can exist free in nature and may be composed of similar or different atoms. It shows all the properties of that particular substance. For example, H2, N2, O2, H2O, etc.\n\nClassification by Atomicity (Number of Atoms):\n1. Monoatomic Molecule (mono = one): Made up of only one atom (e.g., noble gases He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe).\n2. Diatomic Molecule (di = two): Consists of two atoms (e.g., H2, O2, N2, HCl, CO).\n3. Triatomic Molecule (tri = three): Consists of three atoms (e.g., H2O, CO2, O3).\n4. Polyatomic Molecule (poly = many): Made up of many atoms (e.g., H2SO4, C12H22O11, S8, P4).\n5. Macromolecules (macro = very large): Giant molecules containing thousands of atoms (e.g., Haemoglobin in blood, DNA, starch).\n\nClassification by Nature of Atoms (Table 1.9, Page 28):\n• Homoatomic Molecule (Homo = Same): Molecule consisting of atoms of the same element (also called homonuclear molecule) e.g., O2, H2, Cl2, O3, S8.\n• Heteroatomic Molecule (Hetero = Different): Molecule consisting of atoms of different elements chemically bonded (also called heteronuclear molecule) e.g., CO2, H2O, HCl, NH3, CH4.",
