              "num": "1.5.1",
              "name": "Avogadro's Number (NA), Mole & Molar Masses (Tables 1.10–1.12)",
              "desc": "1.4 Avogadro's Number and Mole:\n1.4.1 Avogadro's Number:\nA chemist deals with substances which are composed of large number of particles. It is not possible for the chemist to count these particles. The concept of Avogadro's number helped in the counting of these particles present in the given mass of a substance. Avogadro's number is collection of 6.023 × 10^23 particles.\nAvogadro's number is defined as the number of particles (atoms, molecules, ions) in one mole of any substance and is numerically equal to 6.023 × 10^23. Represented by symbol NA (discovered by Amedeo Avogadro).\n\nExamples:\n• 1.0 g of Hydrogen = 1 mole = 6.023 × 10^23 atoms of Hydrogen\n• 12 g of Carbon = 1 mole = 6.023 × 10^23 atoms of Carbon\n• 18 g of Water = 1 mole = 6.023 × 10^23 molecules of Water\n• 58.5 g of NaCl = 1 mole = 6.023 × 10^23 formula units of NaCl\n\nSociety, Technology and Science (Page 29):\n• If there were a mole of rice grains, all the land area in the whole world would be covered with rice to a depth of about 75 meters.\n• One mole of rice grains is more than all the grain that has been grown since the beginning of time.\n• A computer counting with a speed of 10 million atoms a second, would take two billion years to count one mole of atoms.\n• If one mole of marbles were spread over the surface of the Earth, our planet would be covered by a three miles thick layer of marbles.\n\n1.4.2 Mole:\nChemists use a counting unit called mole to measure the amount of a substance.\nA mole is defined as the amount (mass) of a substance which contains Avogadro's number (6.023 × 10^23 particles). Symbol: 'mol'.\nQuantitative definition: The atomic mass, molecular mass or formula mass of a substance expressed in grams is called mole (or molar mass):\n• Atomic mass of Carbon = 12 amu → 12 g = 1 mole of Carbon\n• Molecular mass of H2O = 18 amu → 18 g = 1 mole of Water\n• Molecular mass of H2SO4 = 98 amu → 98 g = 1 mole of Sulphuric acid\n• Formula mass of NaCl = 58.5 amu → 58.5 g = 1 mole of Sodium chloride\n\n1.4.3 Gram Atomic Mass, Gram Molecular Mass and Gram Formula Mass:\n(i) Gram Atomic Mass (Table 1.10): Relative atomic mass expressed in grams (1 gram atom = 1 mole of atoms):\n• 1 gram atom of Hydrogen = 1.008 g = 1 mole of H atoms\n• 1 gram atom of Oxygen = 15.998 g = 1 mole of O atoms\n• 1 gram atom of Carbon = 12.00 g = 1 mole of C atoms\n\n(ii) Gram Molecular Mass (Table 1.11): Molecular mass of a substance expressed in grams (1 gram molecule = 1 mole of molecules):\n• Water (H2O) = 18 g (1 mole)\n• Carbon dioxide (CO2) = 44 g (1 mole)\n• Sulphuric acid (H2SO4) = 98 g (1 mole)\n\n(iii) Gram Formula Mass (Table 1.12): Formula mass of an ionic compound expressed in grams (1 gram formula = 1 mole of formula units):\n• Sodium Chloride (NaCl) = 58.5 g (1 mole)\n• Calcium Chloride (CaCl2) = 111 g (1 mole)",
              "sciNote": "One mole of any ideal gas at standard temperature and pressure (STP: 0 °C, 1 atm) occupies a constant molar volume of 22.414 dm³ (litres).",
              "sq": "Define a mole quantitatively and qualitatively with two examples.",
