              "num": "2.2.1",
              "name": "Shells, Sub-shells (s, p, d, f) & Aufbau Principle",
              "desc": "2.2 Electronic Configuration:\nThe distribution or arrangement of electrons around the nucleus in orbits or shells is called the electronic configuration.\nThe electrons are distributed around the nucleus in various orbits or energy levels or shells. These orbits are numbered as 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7... and represented by K, L, M, N etc.\nThe maximum number of electrons in a particular orbit is given by the formula 2n^2 (where n = 1, 2, 3, 4...):\n\nTable 2.2: Orbits and Maximum Number of Electrons (Page 48):\n• n = 1 → K shell: 2(1)^2 = 2 electrons\n• n = 2 → L shell: 2(2)^2 = 8 electrons\n• n = 3 → M shell: 2(3)^2 = 18 electrons\n• n = 4 → N shell: 2(4)^2 = 32 electrons\n• n = 5 → O shell: 2(5)^2 = 50 electrons\n\n2.2.1 Concepts of s and p sub-shell:\n(i) Shell (Orbit): Fixed circular paths described by 'n' value. As 'n' increases, distance from nucleus and energy increase.\n(ii) Sub-Shell (sub-orbit): One or more orbitals within an electron shell of an atom with the same energy level. Spectroscopic designations:\n• s = sharp (1 orbital, max 2 electrons, spherical shape)\n• p = principal (3 orbitals: px, py, pz, max 6 electrons, dumb-bell shape)\n• d = diffused (5 orbitals, max 10 electrons, complex double dumb-bell shape)\n• f = fundamental (7 orbitals, max 14 electrons, complex multi-lobed shape)\n\nTable 2.3: Shells, Maximum Electrons & Sub-shells (Page 50):\n• K-shell (n = 1): Max 2 electrons → Sub-shell: s (1s^2)\n• L-shell (n = 2): Max 8 electrons → Sub-shells: s and p (2s^2, 2p^6)\n• M-shell (n = 3): Max 18 electrons → Sub-shells: s, p, d (3s^2, 3p^6, 3d^10)\n• N-shell (n = 4): Max 32 electrons → Sub-shells: s, p, d, f (4s^2, 4p^6, 4d^10, 4f^14)\n\nIncreasing order of sub-shell energy:\n1s < 2s < 2p < 3s < 3p < 4s < 3d < 4p < 5s < 4d < 5p < 6s < 4f < 5d < 6p...\n\nAufbau Principle:\nAccording to the Aufbau principle (German 'building up'), electrons fill first the lowest energy sub-shell available to it and then successively higher energy sub-shells. Therefore, electrons fill 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s and then 3d, because the energy of 4s is lower than 3d.",
              "sciNote": "The (n + l) rule determines subshell energy: for 4s, (n + l) = 4 + 0 = 4; for 3d, (n + l) = 3 + 2 = 5. Since 4 < 5, 4s fills before 3d.",
