            "q": "(iv) Define shielding effect and draw its effects upon the ionization energy, electron affinity and electronegativity.",
            "ans": "Answer:\n1. Definition of Shielding Effect (Screening Effect):\nThe phenomenon in which inner core electron shells repel the outer valence electrons and screen them from the full positive attractive pull of the nucleus is called the Shielding Effect.\n\n2. Impact of Shielding Effect on Periodic Properties:\n• On Ionization Energy (IE): Inversely proportional. Greater shielding down a group weakens nuclear attraction on valence electrons, causing Ionization Energy to DECREASE.\n• On Electron Affinity (EA): Inversely proportional. Greater shielding reduces the nucleus's ability to attract an incoming extra electron, causing Electron Affinity to DECREASE (become less negative).\n• On Electronegativity (EN): Inversely proportional. Increased shielding screens the positive nucleus from attracting shared bonded electron pairs, causing Electronegativity to DECREASE.\n\n3. Summary Trend Chart (Fig 3.9):\n• Down a Group: Shielding increases → IE decreases, EA decreases, EN decreases, Atomic radius increases.\n• Across a Period: Shielding remains constant while Nuclear Charge increases → IE increases, EA increases, EN increases, Atomic radius decreases."
