            "q": "(ii) (a) What is the main difference between ionic and covalent bonding? Explain your answer with suitable examples. (b) How is electronegativity used in determining the ionic or covalent character of the bonding between two elements?",
            "ans": "(a) Difference between Ionic and Covalent Bonding:\n1. Mechanism of Formation:\n• Ionic Bond: Formed by the complete transfer of one or more electrons from a metallic atom (electropositive) to a non-metallic atom (electronegative).\nExample: Formation of Sodium Chloride (NaCl). Sodium (2,8,1) transfers its 1 valence electron to Chlorine (2,8,7), producing Na+ (2,8) and Cl- (2,8,8). Electrostatic attraction between Na+ and Cl- forms the ionic crystal.\n• Covalent Bond: Formed by the mutual sharing of valence electrons between two non-metallic atoms of similar or moderate electronegativity.\nExample: Formation of Chlorine molecule (Cl2). Each Cl atom (2,8,7) shares 1 electron with the other, forming a single shared electron pair (Cl─Cl).\n\n2. Nature of Units:\n• Ionic compounds exist as three-dimensional crystalline lattices of cations and anions (no discrete molecules).\n• Covalent compounds exist as discrete, individual molecules.\n\n3. Physical Properties:\n• Ionic compounds have high melting/boiling points and conduct electricity in molten/solution states.\n• Covalent compounds typically have lower melting/boiling points and are non-electrolytes.\n\n(b) Role of Electronegativity in Determining Bond Character:\nElectronegativity (EN) is the measure of the ability of an atom in a molecule to attract the shared pair of electrons towards itself. The difference in electronegativity (ΔEN) between the two bonded atoms determines the nature of the bond:\n1. If ΔEN > 1.7: The bond is predominantly Ionic. One atom has a far stronger attraction for electrons, resulting in complete electron transfer (e.g. NaCl: 3.0 - 0.9 = 2.1 > 1.7).\n2. If 0.5 <= ΔEN <= 1.7: The bond is Polar Covalent. Electrons are shared unequally, producing partial positive (δ+) and partial negative (δ-) poles (e.g. HCl: 3.0 - 2.1 = 0.9; H2O: 3.5 - 2.1 = 1.4).\n3. If ΔEN < 0.5: The bond is Non-Polar Covalent. Electrons are shared equally between the two atoms (e.g. H2: ΔEN = 0.0; CH4: 2.5 - 2.1 = 0.4)."
