          "q": "1. (SLO) Why is the melting point of sodium chloride (801°C) much higher than that of carbon tetrachloride (-23°C)?",
          "ans": "Sodium chloride (NaCl) is an ionic compound composed of Na+ and Cl- ions held together by extremely strong electrostatic forces throughout a 3D crystal lattice, requiring large amounts of thermal energy to overcome (801°C). In contrast, carbon tetrachloride (CCl4) is a non-polar covalent molecular substance where discrete molecules are held together only by weak intermolecular Van der Waals dispersion forces, which require very little thermal energy to break (-23°C)."
