      "sloLq": [
        {
          "q": "1. (SLO) Comprehensive Analysis: Explain the physical states, electrical conductivities, boiling points, and solubility patterns of Ionic, Covalent, and Metallic substances in terms of their internal bonding and intermolecular forces.",
          "ans": "1. Ionic Substances:\n• Internal Bonding: Strong electrostatic attraction between alternating cations and anions in a rigid 3D lattice.\n• Physical State: Hard, crystalline solids at room temperature with high melting/boiling points (e.g. NaCl mp 801°C, MgO mp 2800°C).\n• Electrical Conductivity: Non-conductors in solid state (ions fixed); excellent electrolytes in molten or aqueous solution (ions mobile).\n• Solubility: Highly soluble in polar solvents (water) due to ion-dipole hydration; insoluble in non-polar organic solvents.\n\n2. Covalent Substances:\n• Internal Bonding: Shared electron pairs within discrete molecules (strong intramolecular bonds) with weak intermolecular forces (Van der Waals / H-bonds) between molecules.\n• Physical State: Gases (O2, CH4), volatile liquids (Br2, H2O), or low-melting solids (I2, sugar).\n• Electrical Conductivity: Non-electrolytes in both pure and aqueous forms (no free ions or mobile electrons).\n• Solubility: 'Like dissolves like' ── polar covalent compounds (e.g. alcohol, glucose) dissolve in polar water; non-polar covalent compounds (e.g. grease, benzene) dissolve in non-polar solvents (CCl4, ether).\n\n3. Metallic Substances:\n• Internal Bonding: Attraction between positive metal kernels and a mobile sea of delocalized electrons.\n• Physical State: Malleable, ductile, lustrous solids at room temperature (except liquid Mercury) with moderate to high melting points.\n• Electrical & Thermal Conductivity: Excellent conductors in both solid and liquid states due to freely moving delocalized electrons.\n• Solubility: Insoluble in both polar and non-polar solvents; some react chemically with acids/water or dissolve in other molten metals to form alloys."
        }
      ],
      "definitions": [
        {
          "term": "Chemical Bond",
          "def": "The attractive force which holds the atoms or ions together to form a molecule or compound."
        },
        {
          "term": "Octet Rule (Rule of Eight)",
          "def": "The tendency of atoms to attain eight electrons in their outermost valence shell in order to achieve maximum chemical stability."
        },
        {
          "term": "Duplet Rule (Rule of Two)",
          "def": "The tendency of atoms (such as H, Li, Be) to attain two electrons in their valence shell, achieving the stable electronic configuration of Helium."
        },
        {
          "term": "Ionic Bond (Electrovalent Bond)",
          "def": "The chemical bond formed due to the complete transfer of one or more electrons from one atom (metal) to another atom (non-metal)."
        },
        {
          "term": "Covalent Bond",
          "def": "The chemical bond formed by the mutual sharing of valence electrons between two atoms."
        },
        {
          "term": "Coordinate Covalent (Dative) Bond",
          "def": "A type of covalent bond in which the shared pair of electrons is donated by only one of the bonded atoms (the donor) to an electron-deficient species (the acceptor)."
        },
        {
          "term": "Polar Covalent Bond",
          "def": "A covalent bond formed between atoms with different electronegativities (ΔEN > 0.5), causing unequal electron sharing and partial charges (δ+ and δ-)."
        },
        {
          "term": "Non-Polar Covalent Bond",
          "def": "A covalent bond formed between atoms of identical or similar electronegativities (ΔEN < 0.5) where electrons are shared equally."
        },
        {
          "term": "Metallic Bond",
          "def": "The chemical bonding that results from the electrostatic attraction between positively charged metal ions and the surrounding sea of mobile delocalized electrons."
        },
        {
          "term": "Intermolecular Forces",
          "def": "The attractive forces acting between separate molecules of a substance, collectively known as Van der Waals forces."
        },
        {
          "term": "Hydrogen Bond",
          "def": "The attractive force between a highly electron-deficient hydrogen atom covalently bonded to F, O, or N in one molecule and a lone pair on a nearby F, O, or N atom."
        },
        {
          "term": "Malleability & Ductility",
          "def": "Malleability is the property of metals to be hammered into thin sheets; Ductility is the property to be drawn into wires without breaking."
        }
      ],
