              "desc": "Some typical properties of gaseous state are following:\n\ni. Indefinite Volume:\nGases have no definite volume and they occupy all the available space.\n\nii. Indefinite Shape:\nGases do not have shape but take the shape of the container in which it is placed.\n\niii. Diffusion:\nDiffusion describes the movement of molecules from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration. In other words, the molecules tend to move from a more crowded region to a less crowded region. Once the molecules become evenly distributed throughout the medium, there will be no net diffusion in any particular direction.\n\nGas molecules are in constant random motion. Because of this motion, the gas molecules diffuse very quickly and form a homogenous mixture. The spontaneous mixing of the molecules of different gases to form a homogenous mixture by random motion and collision of the molecules is called diffusion. For example, when a gas having characteristic odour is released at one corner of the room, soon its smell will spread in the whole room. Similarly, a volatile liquid (perfume) evaporates and spreads in the air in a very short time. The rate of diffusion of gases varies from gas to gas. In 1833, Thomas Graham an English chemist, discovered that a lighter gas can diffuse much faster than a heavier gas.\n[Fig. 5.1 Diffusion of Perfume Molecules in Air: Perfume molecules disperse randomly among nitrogen and oxygen molecules in air].\n\niv. Effusion:\nWhen a gas is allowed to pass through a hole of the size of a molecule, only one molecule at a time can pass through the hole. The escape of molecules in the gaseous state one by one without collision through a hole of molecular dimension is called effusion. You can smell the onions even when the bag is tightly sealed, due to effusion.\n[Fig. 5.2 Effusion of onion's smell; Fig. 5.3 Effusion of gases through a pinhole].\n\nActivity: 5.1\nInvert a jar containing reddish brown coloured bromine vapours, over a jar of air. The reddish brown colour spreads inside the jar of air, demonstrating rapid gas diffusion.\n\nPoint to Ponder:\nWhy a gas filled balloon floating up in the room, comes down after a day or two?\nAnswer: The helium or air molecules inside the balloon effuse out slowly through the microscopic pores in the rubber membrane of the balloon, causing it to lose gas volume and density over time.",
