              "desc": "Concentration is the proportion of a solute in a solution. The concentration of a solution is a measure or ratio of the amount of solute in a given amount of solvent or solution. The larger the amount of solute present in a solution, the higher would be the concentration.\n\n(i) Dilute solution:\nA solution which contains a relatively small amount of solute dissolved in the solvent is called a dilute solution.\n\n(ii) Concentrated solution:\nA solution which contains a relatively large amount of solute dissolved in the solvent is called a concentrated solution.\n\nScientific Information:\nA solution of known concentration is called a standard solution. For example, a solution in which 5 g of sugar is dissolved in 100 cm^3 of water is dilute compared to a solution in which 8 g of sugar is dissolved in 100 cm^3 of water.\n[Fig. 6.6 Dilution of Solution: Adding solvent increases volume and decreases solute particles per unit volume].\n\n6.4.1 Percentage Composition:\nPercentage composition is the number of parts of solute present in 100 parts of solution, or the fraction of a solute in a solution multiplied by 100.\n\nThe percentage composition of a solution is expressed in four ways:\n\ni. Percentage Mass by Mass (% m/m):\nThe number of grams by mass of solute present in 100 grams by mass of solution.\n% m/m = [Mass of solute (g) / Mass of solution (g)] × 100\nWhere Mass of solution = Mass of solute + Mass of solvent.\nExample: A 10% sugar solution by mass contains 10 g sugar dissolved in 90 g water (total mass = 100 g).\n\nii. Percentage Mass by Volume (% m/v):\nThe number of grams by mass of solute present in 100 cm^3 of solution.\n% m/v = [Mass of solute (g) / Volume of solution (cm^3)] × 100\nExample: A 10% NaCl (m/v) solution contains 10 g NaCl dissolved in sufficient water to make 100 cm^3 total solution.\n\niii. Percentage Volume by Mass (% v/m):\nThe volume in cm^3 of solute dissolved in 100 grams of solution.\n% v/m = [Volume of solute (cm^3) / Mass of solution (g)] × 100\nExample: A 10% alcohol (v/m) solution contains 10 cm^3 alcohol in water such that the total mass of solution is 100 g.\n\niv. Percentage Volume by Volume (% v/v):\nThe volume in cm^3 of solute dissolved per 100 cm^3 of solution.\n% v/v = [Volume of solute (cm^3) / Volume of solution (cm^3)] × 100\nExample: A 10% alcohol (v/v) solution contains 10 cm^3 alcohol in sufficient water to make 100 cm^3 of total solution.",
              "sciNote": "% m/v is widely used in medicine and pharmacology (e.g. 0.9% normal saline contains 0.9 g NaCl per 100 mL solution).",
              "sq": "Define % m/m and % m/v with one example of each.",
