              "num": "6.4.2",
              "name": "Molarity, Preparation of Molar Solutions & Dilution Equation",
              "desc": "6.4.2 Molarity (M):\nMolarity is a concentration unit defined as the number of moles of solute dissolved per dm^3 (liter) of solution. It is represented by 'M'.\n\nMolarity (M) = Number of moles of solute / Volume of solution in dm^3\nSince:\nMoles = Mass of solute in grams / Molecular mass of solute (Molar mass)\nTherefore:\nMolarity (M) = Mass of solute (g) / [Molar mass of solute (g/mol) × Volume of solution (dm^3)]\n\nScientific Information:\n1 dm^3 = 1 liter = 1000 cm^3 = 1000 mL\n1 cm^3 = 1 mL\n\nEXAMPLE: 6.1\nCalculate the molarity of a solution containing 7.50 mol of CaCO3 in enough water to make 1.50 dm^3 of solution.\nSolution:\nMolarity = Moles / Volume = 7.50 mol / 1.50 dm^3 = 5.0 mol/dm^3 = 5.0 M\n\n6.4.3 Problems Involving the Molarity of a Solution:\n• 1 M Glucose (C6H12O6, molar mass = 180 g/mol) contains 180 g glucose in 1 dm^3 solution.\n• 1 M Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH, molar mass = 40.0 g/mol) contains 40.0 g NaOH in 1 dm^3 solution.\n• 0.500 M NaOH contains 20.0 g NaOH in 1 dm^3 solution: M = 20 / (40 × 1) = 0.500 M.\n• 2.0 M NaOH contains 80.0 g NaOH in 1 dm^3 solution: M = 80 / (40 × 1) = 2.0 M.\n\nEXAMPLE: 6.2\n5.85 g of NaCl is dissolved in water so as to make 500 cm^3 solution. Determine the molarity of the solution. (Atomic mass: Na = 23, Cl = 35.5 g/mol).\nSolution:\nMass of NaCl = 5.85 g\nMolar mass of NaCl = 23 + 35.5 = 58.5 g/mol\nMoles of NaCl = 5.85 / 58.5 = 0.1 mol\nVolume of solution = 500 cm^3 / 1000 = 0.5 dm^3\nMolarity (M) = 0.1 mol / 0.5 dm^3 = 0.2 M\n\nEXAMPLE: 6.3\nCalculate the molarity of 50.0 cm^3 of solution containing 7.50 g of CH3OH (Molar mass = 32.0 g/mol).\nSolution:\nMoles of CH3OH = 7.50 g / 32.0 g/mol = 0.2344 mol\nVolume = 50.0 cm^3 / 1000 = 0.0500 dm^3\nMolarity (M) = 0.2344 mol / 0.0500 dm^3 = 4.69 M\n\nPractice Problem: 6.1\nCalculate the molarity of 11.6 cm^3 of solution containing 0.750 g of CaCl2 (Molar mass of CaCl2 = 40 + 2(35.5) = 111 g/mol).\nSolution:\nMoles of CaCl2 = 0.750 g / 111 g/mol = 0.006757 mol\nVolume = 11.6 cm^3 / 1000 = 0.0116 dm^3\nMolarity (M) = 0.006757 mol / 0.0116 dm^3 = 0.582 M\n\nActivity: 6.1: Preparation of 0.5000 M solution of CuSO4·5H2O:\n1. Molar mass of CuSO4·5H2O = 63.5 + 32 + 4(16) + 5(18) = 249.5 g/mol.\n2. Mass needed for 1.0 dm^3 of 0.5000 M = 0.5000 mol × 249.5 g/mol = 124.8 g.\n3. Dissolve 124.8 g in some distilled water in a beaker, transfer into a 1.0 dm^3 volumetric flask, rinse beaker, fill up to neck, and adjust meniscus to the 1.0 dm^3 mark. Stopper and invert 10 times.\n\nDilution of Solutions:\nScientific Information: Dilution Equation: M1V1 = M2V2\nWhere M1 and V1 are molarity and volume of concentrated stock solution, and M2 and V2 are molarity and volume of diluted solution.\n\nEXAMPLE: 6.4\nWhat will be the molarity of the solution if 30 cm^3 of 2.5 M of CaCO3 solution is diluted to 280 cm^3?\nSolution: M1 = 2.5 M, V1 = 30 cm^3, V2 = 280 cm^3, M2 = ?\nM2 = (M1 × V1) / V2 = (2.5 × 30) / 280 = 75 / 280 = 0.267 M\n\nEXAMPLE: 6.5\nWhat volume of water must be added to 85 cm^3 of 3.5 M Na2CO3 to dilute it to 0.41 M?\nSolution: M1 = 3.5 M, V1 = 85 cm^3, M2 = 0.41 M, V2 = ?\nV2 = (M1 × V1) / M2 = (3.5 × 85) / 0.41 = 297.5 / 0.41 = 725.60 cm^3\nVolume of water to add = V2 - V1 = 725.60 - 85 = 640.60 cm^3",
              "sciNote": "During dilution, the amount of solute in moles remains strictly constant (moles before = moles after); only solvent volume increases.",
