          "q": "1. (SLO) Comprehensive Investigation: Compare True Solutions, Colloidal Dispersions, and Suspensions across 7 analytical criteria (homogeneity, particle size, appearance, filtration, stability, sedimentation, and Tyndall effect).",
          "ans": "1. Homogeneity: True Solutions are homogeneous single-phase mixtures; Colloids and Suspensions are heterogeneous two-phase systems.\n2. Particle Size: Solutions (<1 nm, atomic/ionic/molecular level); Colloids (1 - 1000 nm, macromolecular aggregates); Suspensions (>1000 nm, coarse visible particles).\n3. Optical Appearance: Solutions are transparent and clear; Colloids are translucent or cloudy; Suspensions are turbid and opaque.\n4. Separation by Ordinary Filtration: Solutions (passes completely, 0 residue); Colloids (passes completely, 0 residue); Suspensions (particles retained on filter paper as residue).\n5. Separation by Ultrafiltration / Membranes: Solutions pass through semi-permeable membranes; Colloids are stopped by ultrafilter membranes; Suspensions are stopped.\n6. Stability on Standing (Gravity): Solutions and Colloids never settle under gravity; Suspensions settle rapidly by sedimentation.\n7. Tyndall Effect: Solutions show no Tyndall effect (light path invisible); Colloids scatter light brilliantly (Tyndall cone); Suspensions block, absorb, or reflect light without true Tyndall scattering."
