              "name": "Concepts of Oxidation, Reduction & Redox Reactions",
              "desc": "Electrons are lost in oxidation and gained in reduction. Therefore, when oxidation occurs during a chemical reaction, reduction must occur simultaneously. The number of electrons lost in oxidation must equal the number of electrons gained in reduction. This combined reaction is often shortened to redox reaction.\n\nOxidation:\na) The addition of oxygen to a substance is called oxidation. For example, carbon is oxidized to CO2 by the addition of oxygen:\nC + O2 ──> CO2\n\nb) The removal of hydrogen or other electropositive elements from a substance is called oxidation. For example, ammonia (NH3) is oxidized to nitrogen by losing hydrogen:\n2NH3 + 3Cl2 ──> N2 + 6HCl\n\nc) The removal of electron(s) from a substance is also called oxidation. For example, when Fe2+ loses an electron and oxidizes to Fe3+:\nFe2+ ──> Fe3+ + e-\n\nReduction:\na) The removal of oxygen from a substance is called reduction. For example, mercuric oxide (HgO) is reduced to mercury on heating:\n2HgO ──(Heat)──> 2Hg + O2\n\nScientific Information:\nFireflies contain special cells in their abdomens that produce light. These cells contain a chemical called luciferin, which undergoes oxidation and produces light when the firefly takes in oxygen.\n\nb) The addition of hydrogen to a substance is called reduction. For example, nitrogen is reduced to ammonia (NH3):\nN2 + 3H2 ──> 2NH3\n\nc) The addition of electron(s) to a substance is also called reduction. For example, Sn4+ gains two electrons and reduces to Sn2+:\nSn4+ + 2e- ──> Sn2+\n\nConsider the reaction between sodium (Na) and chlorine (Cl2). This reaction involves the transfer of electrons from sodium atoms to chlorine atoms. Sodium atom loses an electron and is oxidized to sodium ion (Na+), while chlorine atom gains an electron and is reduced to chloride ion (Cl-). Those reactions in which gain and loss of electrons take place simultaneously are called oxidation-reduction or Redox reactions:\n2Na + Cl2 ──> 2NaCl",
