              "num": "7.3.1",
              "name": "Oxidizing and Reducing Agents & Redox Half-Reactions",
              "desc": "7.3 Oxidizing and Reducing Agents:\n(i) Oxidizing Agent:\nAn oxidizing agent is the species that oxidizes another substance and is itself reduced in the process. Examples: KMnO4, K2Cr2O7, HNO3, conc. H2SO4, Cl2, Br2, O2, Ozone (O3).\nAn oxidizing agent:\n• Supplies oxygen to another substance.\n• Removes hydrogen from another substance.\n• Accepts electrons from another substance.\n• Undergoes a decrease in its oxidation number during a redox reaction.\n\nEXAMPLE: 7.4\nZn + CuO ──> ZnO + Cu\nOxidation number of Cu in CuO is +2 and in Cu(s) is 0 (decrease in oxidation number). Thus, CuO is the Oxidizing Agent, and Zn is the Reducing Agent.\n\nEXAMPLE: 7.5\nSn2+ + Cl2 ──> Sn4+ + 2Cl-\nOxidation number of Cl2 is 0 and in Cl- is -1 (decrease). Cl2 is the Oxidizing Agent, and Sn2+ is the Reducing Agent.\n\n(ii) Reducing Agent:\nA reducing agent is the species that reduces another substance and is itself oxidized in the process. Examples: H2S, SO2, Na, Al, Mg, Zn, C, CO, KI.\nA reducing agent:\n• Accepts oxygen from another substance.\n• Donates hydrogen to another substance.\n• Donates electrons to another substance.\n• Undergoes an increase in its oxidation number during a redox reaction.\n\nReaction: Br2 + H2S ──> 2HBr + S\nOxidation number of S in H2S is -2 and in free S is 0 (increase). Thus, H2S is the Reducing Agent, and Br2 is the Oxidizing Agent.\n\nTable 7.1: Examples of Oxidizing and Reducing Agents:\n• Oxidizing Agents: Bromine (Br2), Chlorine (Cl2), Conc. Sulfuric acid (H2SO4), Nitric acid (HNO3), Oxygen (O2), Potassium permanganate (KMnO4), Potassium dichromate (K2Cr2O7).\n• Reducing Agents: Carbon (C), Carbon monoxide (CO), Hydrogen (H2), Hydrogen sulphide (H2S), Metals (Na, Mg, Zn), Potassium iodide (KI), Sulphur dioxide (SO2).\n\nScientific Information:\nOzone (O3) is a powerful oxidizing agent used in bleaching and water purification. Wet oxidation involves dissolving oxygen at high temperature and pressure in industrial wastewater to decompose toxic organic pollutants.\n\n7.4 Oxidation - Reduction Reactions:\nRedox reactions consist of two simultaneous half-reactions:\nCuSO4(aq) + Zn(s) ──> ZnSO4(aq) + Cu(s)\nNet ionic equation: Cu2+(aq) + Zn(s) ──> Zn2+(aq) + Cu(s)\n• Oxidation Half-Reaction: Zn(s) ──> Zn2+(aq) + 2e- (loss of electrons at anode)\n• Reduction Half-Reaction: Cu2+(aq) + 2e- ──> Cu(s) (gain of electrons at cathode)\n[Fig. 7.2 Comprehensive Redox Concept Map].",
