              "desc": "8.1.2 Alkali Metals:\nThe elements of Group IA except Hydrogen are called alkali metals. The name alkali comes from the Arabic 'Al-Qali' meaning 'the Ashes', as they were first extracted from plant ashes. They react with water to form strong alkalies.\nAlkali metals include: Lithium (Li), Sodium (Na), Potassium (K), Rubidium (Rb), Cesium (Cs), and Francium (Fr) [Fig. 8.1].\n\nCharacteristics:\n• Valence shell configuration: ns^1 with one valence electron.\n• Low ionization energies; lose 1 electron to form monopositive M+ cations.\n• Form ionic compounds with Group VIA (oxides, sulphides) and Group VIIA (halides).\n\nOccurrence:\nVery reactive; never found free in nature. Lithium occurs as spodumene [LiAl(SiO3)2]. Sodium and Potassium constitute 2.4% of the Earth's crust each. Rubidium and Cesium occur in potassium salt deposits. Francium is radioactive and prepared synthetically.\n\nTable 8.1: Reactivity of Alkali Metals (Reactivity Order: Li < Na < K < Rb < Cs):\n1. Lithium (Li, At. No = 3):\n• Reaction with Air/Oxygen: Burns with a red flame to give white solid lithium oxide:\n  4Li(s) + O2(g) ──> 2Li2O(s)\n• Reaction with Water: Floats on water and reacts quickly to produce lithium hydroxide and hydrogen gas:\n  2Li(s) + 2H2O(l) ──> 2LiOH(aq) + H2(g)\n• Reaction with Chlorine: Burns with a bright flame to give white solid lithium chloride:\n  2Li(s) + Cl2(g) ──> 2LiCl(s)\n\n2. Sodium (Na, At. No = 11):\n• Reaction with Air/Oxygen: Burns with a bright yellow flame to produce white sodium oxide:\n  4Na(s) + O2(g) ──> 2Na2O(s)\n• Reaction with Water: Floats on water and reacts very vigorously, melting into a silvery sphere, releasing hydrogen gas which may ignite:\n  2Na(s) + 2H2O(l) ──> 2NaOH(aq) + H2(g) + Heat\n• Reaction with Chlorine: Burns with a bright flame to give white sodium chloride:\n  2Na(s) + Cl2(g) ──> 2NaCl(s)\n\n3. Potassium (K, At. No = 19):\n• Reaction with Air/Oxygen: Burns violently with a violet / lilac-colored flame to produce white potassium oxide:\n  4K(s) + O2(g) ──> 2K2O(s)\n• Reaction with Water: Floats on water and reacts explosively with a lilac flame, producing potassium hydroxide and hydrogen gas:\n  2K(s) + 2H2O(l) ──> 2KOH(aq) + H2(g)\n• Reaction with Chlorine: Burns vigorously in chlorine with a bright flame forming potassium chloride:\n  2K(s) + Cl2(g) ──> 2KCl(s)\n\nScientific Information:\nAlkali metals react violently with atmospheric moisture and oxygen, so they are stored submerged under kerosene oil or liquid paraffin.",
