              "num": "8.3.1",
              "name": "Alkaline Earth Metals & Ionization Energy Comparisons",
              "desc": "8.1.3 Alkaline Earth Metals:\nThe elements of Group IIA are called alkaline earth metals because their oxides form alkaline solutions and are widely distributed in the Earth's crust.\nAlkaline earth metals include: Beryllium (Be), Magnesium (Mg), Calcium (Ca), Strontium (Sr), Barium (Ba), and Radium (Ra).\n• Valence shell configuration: ns^2 with two valence electrons.\n• Form divalent M2+ cations by losing both valence electrons.\n• Abundant minerals: Gypsum (CaSO4·2H2O), Epsom salt (MgSO4·7H2O), Barite (BaSO4), Dolomite [CaCO3·MgCO3], Beryl [Be3Al2(SiO3)6], Chlorophyll (Mg in green plants), and skeletal bones/teeth/shells (Ca).\n\nIonization Energy Comparison: Group IA vs Group IIA:\nIonization energy is the minimum energy required to remove an electron from the outermost shell of an isolated gaseous atom (kJ/mol).\n\n• Group IA (Alkali Metals, Table 8.2):\n  Li = 520, Na = 496, K = 419, Rb = 403, Cs = 376 kJ/mol.\n  Alkali metals have low 1st ionization energies because they need to lose only one s-electron. IE decreases down the group as atomic radius increases.\n\n• Group IIA (Alkaline Earth Metals, Table 8.3):\n  - 1st Ionization Energy (M ──> M+ + e-): Be = 899, Mg = 738, Ca = 590, Sr = 549, Ba = 503 kJ/mol.\n  - 2nd Ionization Energy (M+ ──> M2+ + e-): Be = 1757.1, Mg = 1450.7, Ca = 1145, Sr = 1064.2, Ba = 965.1 kJ/mol.\n\nWhy Group IIA Ionization Energy is Higher:\n1. Group IIA atoms have higher nuclear charge (+2) and smaller atomic radii than corresponding Group IA atoms.\n2. The 2nd electron is removed from a positively charged cation (M+), where the remaining electrons experience much greater effective nuclear attraction (e.g. Mg ──> Mg+ requires 738 kJ/mol, but Mg+ ──> Mg2+ requires 1450.7 kJ/mol).\n\nComparison Summary:\nAlkali metals (Group IA) have lower IE values than Alkaline Earth metals (Group IIA), making alkali metals softer, lighter, and chemically more reactive.",
