              "num": "8.3.2",
              "name": "Magnesium & Calcium: Properties, Reactions & Uses",
              "desc": "Magnesium (Mg, At. No = 12, Table 8.5):\nSilvery-grey solid, density 1.74 g/cm^3, mp 651°C, bp 1106°C, malleable and ductile, good conductor.\n\nCalcium (Ca, At. No = 20, Table 8.6, Fig. 8.3):\nSilvery-white solid, density 1.55 g/cm^3, mp 851°C, bp 1487°C, malleable and ductile, good conductor.\n\nChemical Reactions of Magnesium and Calcium:\n1. Reaction with Hydrogen: Forms ionic hydrides on heating:\n   Mg(s) + H2(g) ──(Heat)──> MgH2(s)\n   Ca(s) + H2(g) ──(Heat)──> CaH2(s)\n2. Reaction with Oxygen / Air:\n   • Magnesium burns with a dazzling white flame forming white magnesium oxide (magnesia, MgO):\n     2Mg(s) + O2(g) ──(Heat)──> 2MgO(s)\n   • Calcium burns with a characteristic brick-red flame forming calcium oxide (quicklime, CaO):\n     2Ca(s) + O2(g) ──(Heat)──> 2CaO(s)\n   • Their oxides dissolve in water to form alkaline solutions:\n     MgO(s) + H2O(l) ──> Mg(OH)2(aq)\n     CaO(s) + H2O(l) ──> Ca(OH)2(aq)\n3. Reaction with Nitrogen: Form ionic nitrides on heating:\n   3Mg(s) + N2(g) ──(Heat)──> Mg3N2(s)\n   3Ca(s) + N2(g) ──(Heat)──> Ca3N2(s)\n4. Reaction with Acids: React vigorously with dilute acids liberating hydrogen gas:\n   Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) ──> MgCl2(aq) + H2(g)\n   Mg(s) + H2SO4(aq) ──> MgSO4(aq) + H2(g)\n   Ca(s) + 2HCl(aq) ──> CaCl2(aq) + H2(g)\n   Ca(s) + H2SO4(aq) ──> CaSO4(s) + H2(g)\n5. Reaction with Halogens: Form ionic halides:\n   Mg(s) + Cl2(g) ──> MgCl2(s)\n   Ca(s) + Cl2(g) ──> CaCl2(s)\n   Ca(s) + Br2(l) ──> CaBr2(s)\n\nUses of Magnesium:\ni. Construction of lightweight, high-strength alloys: Duralumin (Al + Cu + Mg + Mn) and Magnalium (Al + Mg) for aircraft, automobiles, and missile parts.\nii. Used in photographic flashlight powders, flares, and pyrotechnic fireworks.\niii. Used as an industrial deoxidizer in metallurgy and in extracting Titanium (Ti) and Uranium (U).\niv. Magnesium oxide (MgO) mixed with clay makes refractory firebricks for furnace linings.\nv. Magnesium sulphate (MgSO4·7H2O / Epsom salt) is used in textiles, paper sizing, medicine, and fertilizers.\n\nUses of Calcium:\ni. Deoxidizer and desulphurizer in steel casting and copper metallurgy.\nii. Used in producing calcium hydride (CaH2, portable hydrogen source) and calcium fluoride (CaF2), and in extracting uranium.\niii. Quicklime (CaO) and slaked lime [Ca(OH)2] are used as agricultural soil neutralizers, water softening agents, pollution control scrubbers, and in glass, paper, sugar, and steel industries.",
              "sciNote": "Magnesium burns with an intense dazzling white flame emitting ultraviolet light, making it the primary ingredient in military illumination flares.",
