              "name": "Reversible and Irreversible Reactions",
              "desc": "In chemical reactions, reactants are converted into products. Reactions are classified into two main types:\n\n1. **Irreversible Reactions:** Reactions in which products do not recombine to form reactants under the same conditions. These reactions proceed only in one direction (forward direction) and go to completion. They are represented by a single arrow (→) pointing from reactants to products.\n*Example:* 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l) (in the presence of electric spark), or 2KClO₃(s) → 2KCl(s) + 3O₂(g) (heated in open vessel).\n\n2. **Reversible Reactions:** Reactions in which the products react together to re-form the original reactants under the same conditions. These reactions never go to completion; instead, they proceed in both forward and reverse directions simultaneously. They are represented by a double half-arrow (⇌) pointing in opposite directions.\n*Example:* N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) ; H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g).",
              "sciNote": "Real-world analogy: When liquid water is kept in a closed bottle at constant temperature, evaporation and condensation happen simultaneously. When the rate of evaporation equals the rate of condensation, dynamic physical equilibrium is achieved: H₂O(l) ⇌ H₂O(g).",
              "examTip": "Always indicate a reversible reaction with a double half-arrow (⇌). A single arrow (→) is strictly for irreversible reactions that go to completion.",
