              "num": "9.1.2",
              "name": "Concept of Dynamic Equilibrium State",
              "desc": "When a reversible reaction takes place in a closed system at constant temperature, initially only reactants are present, so the rate of the forward reaction (Rf) is maximum, while the rate of the reverse reaction (Rr) is zero. As reactants are converted into products, reactant concentrations decrease, slowing down the forward reaction. Simultaneously, product concentrations increase, causing the reverse reaction to begin and accelerate.\n\nEventually, a state is reached where the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction:\n$$\\text{Rate of Forward Reaction } (R_f) = \\text{Rate of Reverse Reaction } (R_r)$$\n\nThis state is called **Dynamic Equilibrium**. Although the reaction appears to have stopped macroscopically because the concentrations of all reactants and products remain constant, microscopically both reactions continue at exactly the same rate in opposite directions.\n\n*Static Equilibrium:* A state where no motion or reaction takes place at all (e.g., a balanced building or a book resting on a table). Chemical systems establish dynamic, not static, equilibrium.",
              "sciNote": "Tug-of-war analogy: If two balanced teams pull a rope with identical forces in opposite directions, the rope does not move. Macroscopically it appears motionless, but high tension and continuous opposing forces exist dynamically.",
              "examTip": "Examiners often ask why dynamic equilibrium is termed 'dynamic'. State clearly: It is dynamic because forward and reverse reactions do NOT stop; they continue indefinitely at identical rates.",
