              "name": "Macroscopic Characteristics of Equilibrium State",
              "desc": "Five fundamental macroscopic characteristics define any chemical system at dynamic equilibrium:\n1. **Closed System Requirement:** Dynamic equilibrium can only be established and maintained in a closed vessel where matter can neither enter nor leave.\n2. **Opposing Rates Equal:** Forward and reverse reactions continue at identical rates (Rf = Rr), meaning reaction never stops.\n3. **Constant Composition:** The amount, concentrations, and physical properties (colour, density, pressure) of all reactants and products remain constant.\n4. **Approach from Either Direction:** Equilibrium can be achieved starting from reactants or starting from products under identical conditions of temperature and pressure.\n5. **Disturbance and Recovery:** An equilibrium state can be disturbed by changing concentration, pressure, volume, or temperature, and under favorable conditions, the system will re-adjust itself to establish a new equilibrium state (Le Chatelier's Principle).",
