              "name": "Ways to Recognize Equilibrium State",
              "desc": "Equilibrium in a chemical system can be identified and verified through two analytical methods:\n\n1. **Physical Methods:**\nIn physical methods, observable physical properties of the system that depend on concentration are measured periodically without disturbing the system:\n- *Spectrophotometry / Colorimetry:* Used when a reactant or product is intensely coloured (e.g., violet vapour of I₂ in H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI). When the colour intensity stops changing, equilibrium is reached.\n- *Pressure Measurement:* In gaseous reactions where mole numbers change, manometers track total pressure until it becomes constant.\n- *Electrical Conductivity / Refractometry:* Tracking electrical conductance in ionic solutions or refractive index until constancy is reached.\n\n2. **Chemical Methods (Titration):**\nSmall samples of the equilibrium mixture are withdrawn at regular time intervals, rapidly 'quenched' (cooled down or diluted to freeze the reaction), and titrated against standard chemical solutions (e.g. standard acid, base, or thiosulphate). When successive titrations yield identical titers, the concentrations have become constant, confirming dynamic equilibrium.",
              "sciNote": "Quenching is a vital laboratory technique: dropping the temperature of an aliquot into ice water instantly slows the forward and reverse reaction rates to near zero, preserving the exact equilibrium composition during titration.",
