              "num": "9.4.2",
              "name": "Predicting the Extent of a Reaction",
              "desc": "The magnitude (numerical value) of $K_c$ indicates how far a reaction proceeds before reaching dynamic equilibrium:\n\n1. **Very Large Value of $K_c$ ($K_c \\gg 1$):**\nA very large $K_c$ means the equilibrium mixture consists almost entirely of products with negligible reactants remaining. The reaction goes almost to completion.\n*Example:* $2CO(g) + O_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2CO_2(g) \\quad (K_c = 2.2 \\times 10^{22} \\text{ at } 1000\\text{ K})$.\n\n2. **Very Small Value of $K_c$ ($K_c \\ll 1$):**\nA very small $K_c$ indicates that only a tiny fraction of reactants converts to products before equilibrium is reached. The equilibrium mixture contains almost entirely reactants. The forward reaction proceeds to a very small extent.\n*Example:* $2H_2O(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2H_2(g) + O_2(g) \\quad (K_c = 4.1 \\times 10^{-48} \\text{ at } 500\\text{ K})$.\n\n3. **Moderate Value of $K_c$ ($K_c \\approx 1$):**\nWhen $K_c$ is neither very large nor very small, comparable amounts of both reactants and products exist in the equilibrium mixture.\n*Example:* $N_2O_4(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NO_2(g) \\quad (K_c = 0.211 \\text{ at } 25^\\circ\\text{C})$, or $H_2(g) + I_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2HI(g) \\quad (K_c = 57.0 \\text{ at } 700\\text{ K})$.",
