              "desc": "While catalysts, concentration, pressure, and volume can shift the position of equilibrium without changing the numerical value of $K_c$, **temperature is the only factor that directly changes the value of $K_c$**:\n\n1. **Exothermic Reactions ($\\Delta H < 0$):** Heat is released. Increasing temperature shifts the equilibrium in the reverse direction, thereby **decreasing $K_c$**. Decreasing temperature increases $K_c$.\n*Example:* $N_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3(g) + \\text{Heat}$. High temperature reduces the value of Kc.\n\n2. **Endothermic Reactions ($\\Delta H > 0$):** Heat is absorbed. Increasing temperature shifts the equilibrium in the forward direction, thereby **increasing $K_c$**. Decreasing temperature decreases $K_c$.\n*Example:* $N_2(g) + O_2(g) + \\text{Heat} \\rightleftharpoons 2NO(g)$. High temperature increases the value of Kc.",
