              "num": "10.1.4",
              "name": "Classification and Strength of Acids and Bases",
              "desc": "Acids and bases are classified by proticity and dissociation strength:\n\n1. **Classification by Proticity:**\n- *Monoprotic (Monobasic) Acids:* Yield 1 proton per molecule upon ionization (e.g. $HCl$, $HNO_3$, $CH_3COOH$).\n- *Diprotic (Dibasic) Acids:* Yield 2 protons per molecule in stages (e.g. $H_2SO_4$, $H_2CO_3$).\n- *Polyprotic (Polybasic) Acids:* Yield 3 or more protons per molecule (e.g. $H_3PO_4$).\n\n2. **Classification by Acidity of Bases:**\n- *Monoacidic Bases:* Produce 1 $OH^-$ ion per formula unit (e.g. $NaOH$, $KOH$).\n- *Diacidic Bases:* Produce 2 $OH^-$ ions per formula unit (e.g. $Ca(OH)_2$, $Mg(OH)_2$).\n- *Polyacidic Bases:* Produce 3 or more $OH^-$ ions per formula unit (e.g. $Al(OH)_3$, $Fe(OH)_3$).\n\n3. **Strength of Acids and Bases:**\n- *Strong Acid:* Ionizes completely in aqueous solution giving high $[H^+]$ (e.g. $HCl$, $HNO_3$, $H_2SO_4$).\n- *Weak Acid:* Ionizes only partially in aqueous solution giving low $[H^+]$ (e.g. $CH_3COOH$, $H_2CO_3$, $HCN$).\n- *Strong Base:* Dissociates completely in aqueous solution giving high $[OH^-]$ (e.g. $NaOH$, $KOH$).\n- *Weak Base:* Dissociates only partially in aqueous solution giving low $[OH^-]$ (e.g. $NH_4OH$, $Mg(OH)_2$).",
