              "num": "10.2.1",
              "name": "Auto-Ionization of Water and Ion-Product Constant (Kw)",
              "desc": "Pure water is an extremely weak electrolyte that undergoes self-ionization (auto-ionization), where one water molecule transfers a proton to another water molecule:\n$$H_2O(l) + H_2O(l) \\rightleftharpoons H_3O^+(aq) + OH^-(aq)$$\nOr in simplified form:\n$$H_2O(l) \\rightleftharpoons H^+(aq) + OH^-(aq)$$\n\nThe equilibrium constant for this ionization is:\n$$K_c = \\frac{[H^+][OH^-]}{[H_2O]}$$\nSince the dissociation of water is extremely small, the concentration of un-ionized water $[H_2O]$ remains virtually constant. Multiplying $K_c$ by $[H_2O]$ yields a new constant called the **Ion-Product Constant of Water ($K_w$)**:\n$$K_w = K_c [H_2O] = [H^+][OH^-]$$\n\nExperimentally, at $25^\\circ\\text{C}$ (298 K):\n$$[H^+] = [OH^-] = 1.0 \\times 10^{-7} \\text{ mol}\\cdot\\text{dm}^{-3}$$\nTherefore:\n$$K_w = (1.0 \\times 10^{-7}) \\times (1.0 \\times 10^{-7}) = 1.0 \\times 10^{-14} \\text{ at } 25^\\circ\\text{C}$$\n\n- In pure neutral water: $[H^+] = [OH^-] = 10^{-7} \\text{ M}$.\n- In acidic solution: $[H^+] > [OH^-]$, so $[H^+] > 10^{-7} \\text{ M}$.\n- In basic solution: $[OH^-] > [H^+]$, so $[H^+] < 10^{-7} \\text{ M}$.",
