              "name": "Definition and General Properties of Salts",
              "desc": "A **salt** is an ionic compound composed of a cation (positive ion, usually a metal ion or ammonium $NH_4^+$) derived from a base, and an anion (negative ion) derived from an acid.\n\n**Characteristics of Salts:**\n1. Form crystalline solids with definite lattice structures.\n2. Have high melting and boiling points due to strong electrostatic ionic bonds.\n3. Most salts are soluble in water; their solutions and molten states conduct electricity due to free mobile ions.\n4. Many salts contain a definite number of water molecules chemically combined in their crystal lattice, known as **water of crystallization** (e.g. Blue vitriol $CuSO_4\\cdot 5H_2O$, Washing soda $Na_2CO_3\\cdot 10H_2O$, Gypsum $CaSO_4\\cdot 2H_2O$).",
