              "name": "Historical Background and Death of Vital Force Theory",
              "desc": "Early chemists believed that chemical compounds were divided into two distinct classes:\n1. **Inorganic Compounds:** Obtained from non-living mineral sources (rocks, minerals, earth).\n2. **Organic Compounds:** Obtained exclusively from living organisms (plants and animals).\n\n**Vital Force Theory:**\nIn 1809, Swedish chemist **Jöns Jacob Berzelius** proposed that organic compounds could only be synthesized within living organisms under the influence of a mysterious, supernatural force called the **'Vital Force'**, and could never be prepared in a laboratory from inorganic materials.\n\n**Demise of Vital Force Theory (Friedrich Wöhler, 1828):**\nIn 1828, German chemist **Friedrich Wöhler** accidentally disproved the Vital Force Theory by heating an inorganic salt, **ammonium cyanate ($NH_4CNO$)**, in his laboratory and obtaining **urea ($NH_2CONH_2$)**, an organic compound found in mammalian urine:\n$$NH_4CNO(s) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} NH_2-CO-NH_2(s) \\quad (\\text{Urea})$$\nThis landmark breakthrough opened the doors of synthetic organic chemistry.",
              "sciNote": "Wöhler wrote excited to Berzelius: 'I must tell you that I can prepare urea without needing kidneys, either of a man or a dog!'.",
              "examTip": "Always write the balanced thermal rearrangement equation of ammonium cyanate to urea when answering questions about the demise of vital force theory.",
