              "num": "11.2.1",
              "name": "General Characteristics of Organic Compounds",
              "desc": "Organic compounds exhibit distinct characteristics that contrast sharply with inorganic minerals:\n1. **Covalent Nature:** Formed predominantly by shared electron pairs (covalent bonds), making them non-ionic.\n2. **Low Melting and Boiling Points:** Intermolecular forces (London dispersion or dipole-dipole) are weaker than ionic crystal lattices; most are gases, volatile liquids, or low-melting solids.\n3. **Solubility ('Like dissolves like'):** Generally insoluble or slightly soluble in polar solvents (water) but highly soluble in non-polar organic solvents (benzene, ether, alcohol, chloroform).\n4. **Combustibility:** Rich in carbon and hydrogen; readily burn in oxygen to produce $CO_2$, $H_2O$, and substantial heat (exothermic).\n5. **Thermal Stability:** Easily decomposed on heating due to covalent bond cleavages.\n6. **Electrical Conductivity:** Poor conductors of electricity in both liquid and solution states due to the absence of free ions.\n7. **Isomerism:** Compounds having identical molecular formulas can possess different structural arrangements and properties.",
              "sciNote": "Why are organic compounds volatile? Because their molecules are held together by weak Van der Waals forces, which require very little thermal energy to break apart into vapours.",
