              "num": "11.2.2",
              "name": "Diversity and Magnitude of Organic Compounds (Catenation)",
              "desc": "Over 10 million organic compounds are known today, far exceeding the number of inorganic compounds. This immense diversity is caused by four unique factors:\n\n1. **Catenation (Self-linking Property):**\nCatenation is the remarkable ability of carbon atoms to link with one another through strong covalent bonds to form long open chains (straight or branched) and closed rings of varying sizes.\n*Two Necessary Conditions for Catenation:*\n- The element must have a valency of two or greater (Carbon has valency 4).\n- The element-element bond ($C-C$, 355 kJ/mol) must be stronger than the element-oxygen bond ($C-O$). In contrast, silicon forms weak $Si-Si$ bonds compared to extremely strong $Si-O$ bonds, so silicon cannot undergo extensive catenation.\n\n2. **Tetravalency of Carbon:**\nWith 4 valence electrons, carbon can form single, double, or triple bonds with carbon and other elements ($H, O, N, S, P$, halogens).\n\n3. **Isomerism:**\nPhenomenon where compounds have identical molecular formulas but different structural formulas (e.g. Butane and Isobutane both share $C_4H_{10}$).\n\n4. **Strength of C-C and C-H Covalent Bonds:**\nDue to small atomic size, carbon nuclei hold shared electron pairs tightly, creating stable, durable molecular backbones.",
