              "desc": "The primary natural sources of organic compounds are fossil fuels formed millions of years ago by the anaerobic decomposition of ancient plant and animal remains buried under high pressure and temperature:\n\n1. **Coal:**\nFormed by the carbonization of dead vegetative trees buried deep inside the earth over 300 million years:\n$$\\text{Wood} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Bacteria/Pressure}} \\text{Peat} (60\\% \\text{ C}) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta, P} \\text{Lignite} (70\\% \\text{ C}) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta, P} \\text{Bituminous} (80\\% \\text{ C}) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta, P} \\text{Anthracite} (90\\% \\text{ C})$$\n\n**Destructive Distillation of Coal:**\nHeating coal in the absence of air at 500–1000°C produces four major fractions:\n- *Coal Gas:* Gaseous mixture of $H_2, CH_4$, and $CO$; used as an industrial and municipal fuel.\n- *Ammoniacal Liquor:* Aqueous solution of ammonia; neutralized with $H_2SO_4$ to produce ammonium sulphate fertilizer.\n- *Coal Tar:* Thick black viscous liquid containing over 200 aromatic compounds (benzene, toluene, phenol, naphthalene, aniline), separated by fractional distillation.\n- *Coke:* Solid 98% pure carbon residue; used as a metallurgical reducing agent in blast furnaces for iron extraction.\n\n2. **Petroleum (Crude Oil):**\nComplex dark viscous liquid mixture of alkanes, cycloalkanes, and aromatic hydrocarbons formed by ancient marine plankton; separated into useful commercial fractions by **fractional distillation**.\n\n3. **Natural Gas:**\nA mixture of low-boiling gaseous hydrocarbons, consisting predominantly of **85–95% Methane ($CH_4$)** along with ethane, propane, and butane; utilized as domestic Sui gas, CNG for automobiles, and industrial feedstock for urea fertilizer.",
