              "desc": "Alkanes are prepared in the laboratory by two standard synthetic methods:\n\n1. **Hydrogenation of Alkenes and Alkynes (Sabatier-Senderens Reaction):**\nUnsaturated alkenes or alkynes react with hydrogen gas in the presence of finely divided **Nickel ($Ni$) catalyst at 250–300°C** (or $Pt/Pd$ at room temperature) to form saturated alkanes:\n$$CH_2=CH_2 + H_2 \\xrightarrow[250-300^\\circ\\text{C}]{Ni} CH_3-CH_3 \\quad (\\text{Ethane})$$\n$$CH \\equiv CH + 2H_2 \\xrightarrow[250-300^\\circ\\text{C}]{Ni} CH_3-CH_3 \\quad (\\text{Ethane})$$\n*Industrial significance:* This same reaction is used in the manufacture of vegetable ghee (banaspati) by hydrogenating liquid vegetable oils.\n\n2. **Reduction of Alkyl Halides:**\nAlkyl halides react with nascent (atomic) hydrogen produced *in situ* by the action of **Zinc metal dust on dilute hydrochloric acid ($Zn + HCl$):**\n$$Zn + 2HCl \\rightarrow ZnCl_2 + 2[H]$$\n$$CH_3-Cl + 2[H] \\xrightarrow{Zn/HCl} CH_4 + HCl \\quad (\\text{Methane})$$\n$$CH_3-CH_2-Br + 2[H] \\xrightarrow{Zn/HCl} CH_3-CH_3 + HBr \\quad (\\text{Ethane})$$",
