              "desc": "Alkenes are unsaturated hydrocarbons containing a carbon-carbon double bond ($-C=C-$). They are called **olefins** (Latin: *oleum facere* = oil-making) because lower members form oily liquids when reacted with halogens. General formula: $C_nH_{2n}$.\n\n- **Bonding:** The double bond consists of one strong **sigma ($\\sigma$) bond** and one weaker, exposed **pi ($\\pi$) bond** formed by lateral overlap of unhybridized p-orbitals. The bond angle around $sp^2$-carbon is $120^\\circ$.\n\n**Laboratory Preparation Methods:**\n\n1. **Dehydration of Alcohols:**\nHeating an alcohol with concentrated sulphuric acid ($H_2SO_4$) at **170–180°C** removes a molecule of water (dehydration):\n$$CH_3-CH_2-OH \\xrightarrow[170^\\circ\\text{C}]{\\text{conc. } H_2SO_4} CH_2=CH_2 + H_2O \\quad (\\text{Ethene})$$\n*(Alternatively passing alcohol vapour over heated alumina $Al_2O_3$ at 350°C).* \n\n2. **Dehydrohalogenation of Alkyl Halides:**\nHeating an alkyl halide with **alcoholic potassium hydroxide ($\text{alc. } KOH$)** removes one hydrogen atom and one halogen atom from adjacent carbons:\n$$CH_3-CH_2-Br + KOH(\\text{alc.}) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} CH_2=CH_2 + KBr + H_2O \\quad (\\text{Ethene})$$\n$$CH_3-CH_2-Cl + KOH(\\text{alc.}) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} CH_2=CH_2 + KCl + H_2O$$",
