              "desc": "**Definition:** Carbohydrates are polyhydroxy aldehydes or polyhydroxy ketones, or complex substances which yield polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones upon hydrolysis. They are composed of carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen, generally represented by the empirical formula **$C_x(H_2O)_y$** (hydrates of carbon).\n\n**Classification of Carbohydrates:**\nBased on the number of sugar units produced on hydrolysis, carbohydrates are classified into three major groups:\n\n1. **Monosaccharides (Simple Sugars):**\n- Cannot be hydrolyzed into simpler sugars.\n- White crystalline solids, sweet in taste, and highly soluble in water.\n- Contain 3 to 9 carbon atoms with either an aldehyde group (Aldose) or a ketone group (Ketose).\n- *Examples:*\n  - **Glucose ($C_6H_{12}O_6$):** An aldohexose known as blood sugar or grape sugar.\n  - **Fructose ($C_6H_{12}O_6$):** A ketohexose found abundantly in ripe fruits and honey (fruit sugar). It is the sweetest natural sugar.\n\n2. **Oligosaccharides:**\n- Yield **2 to 9 monosaccharide molecules** upon acid or enzymatic hydrolysis.\n- White crystalline, sweet-tasting, and soluble in water.\n- **Disaccharides (Yield 2 monosaccharides):**\n  - **Sucrose (Table Sugar, $C_{12}H_{22}O_{11}$):** Hydrolyzes into 1 Glucose + 1 Fructose:\n    $$C_{12}H_{22}O_{11} + H_2O \\xrightarrow{\\text{Invertase}/H^+} C_6H_{12}O_6 (\\text{Glucose}) + C_6H_{12}O_6 (\\text{Fructose})$$\n  - **Maltose (Malt Sugar):** Yields 2 Glucose molecules.\n  - **Lactose (Milk Sugar):** Yields 1 Glucose + 1 Galactose.\n\n3. **Polysaccharides (Complex Carbohydrates):**\n- High molecular weight polymers that yield **hundreds or thousands of monosaccharide units** upon complete hydrolysis. General formula: $(C_6H_{10}O_5)_n$.\n- Amorphous solids, tasteless (non-sugars), and insoluble or sparingly soluble in water.\n- *Examples:*\n  - **Starch:** Plant storage carbohydrate found in wheat, rice, corn, potatoes. Hydrolyzes into glucose.\n  - **Cellulose:** Structural component of plant cell walls (cotton is 95% pure cellulose, wood is 50% cellulose). Insoluble in water and indigestible by humans.\n  - **Glycogen (Animal Starch):** Storage carbohydrate in mammalian liver and muscle tissues.",
