              "num": "13.5.1",
              "name": "Definition and Classification of Vitamins",
              "desc": "**Definition:** Vitamins (derived from *vital amines*) are essential organic micronutrients required in minute quantities in the human diet to maintain normal growth, metabolism, and health, which the human body cannot synthesize in adequate amounts.\n\n**Classification of Vitamins:**\nBased on their solubility, vitamins are divided into two distinct groups:\n\n1. **Fat-Soluble Vitamins (Vitamins A, D, E, and K):**\n- Dissolve in fats and non-polar organic solvents; insoluble in water.\n- Absorbed in the intestine along with dietary lipids.\n- Stored in the **liver and adipose tissue**; excessive intake can lead to toxic accumulation (**hypervitaminosis**).\n\n2. **Water-Soluble Vitamins (Vitamin B-complex and Vitamin C):**\n- Readily dissolve in water; insoluble in fats.\n- Not stored in significant amounts in the body; excess is excreted harmlessly in urine.\n- Must be supplied continuously and regularly in the daily diet.",
