              "name": "Mechanism of Acid Rain Formation",
              "desc": "**Definition of Acid Rain:**\nNormal unpolluted rainwater is naturally slightly acidic with a pH of about **$5.6$** due to dissolution of natural atmospheric carbon dioxide forming weak carbonic acid:\n$$CO_2(g) + H_2O(l) \\rightleftharpoons H_2CO_3(aq)$$\nWhen atmospheric air is contaminated with industrial emissions of sulphur dioxide ($SO_2$) and nitrogen oxides ($NO_x$), these gases react with moisture and oxygen to form strong acids. Any precipitation (rain, snow, fog, dew) having a **$\\text{pH} < 5.6$** is classified as **Acid Rain** (often reaching extreme pH values between $2.0$ and $4.0$).\n\n**Chemical Reactions of Formation:**\n1. **Sulphur Oxides Pathway:**\n   - Combustion of fossil fuels emits $SO_2$:\n     $$S + O_2 \\rightarrow SO_2$$\n   - $SO_2$ dissolves in rain to form sulphurous acid:\n     $$SO_2 + H_2O \\rightarrow H_2SO_3$$\n   - Catalytic oxidation in the presence of dust or sunlight produces sulphur trioxide ($SO_3$), which dissolves to form strong sulphuric acid:\n     $$2SO_2 + O_2 \\rightarrow 2SO_3$$\n     $$SO_3 + H_2O \\rightarrow H_2SO_4 \\quad (\\text{Sulphuric Acid})$$\n\n2. **Nitrogen Oxides Pathway:**\n   - High temperature engine combustion forms nitric oxide ($NO$):\n     $$N_2 + O_2 \\rightarrow 2NO$$\n   - $NO$ is rapidly oxidized in air to nitrogen dioxide ($NO_2$):\n     $$2NO + O_2 \\rightarrow 2NO_2$$\n   - $NO_2$ dissolves in water droplets in the presence of oxygen to form strong nitric acid:\n     $$4NO_2 + 2H_2O + O_2 \\rightarrow 4HNO_3 \\quad (\\text{Nitric Acid})$$",
              "sciNote": "In 1974, a rainstorm in Pitlochry, Scotland registered a pH of 2.4—as acidic as vinegar—caused by industrial air emissions drifting across Western Europe.",
