              "desc": "**Natural Greenhouse Effect:**\nThe greenhouse effect is the natural process by which greenhouse gases in Earth's lower atmosphere absorb and re-emit infrared (thermal) radiation, warming the surface and lower troposphere.\n\n**Mechanism:**\n1. Incoming solar radiation is composed mainly of **short-wavelength visible light and ultraviolet rays**.\n2. The atmosphere is transparent to these short wavelengths; approximately $50\\%$ passes through and is absorbed by Earth's land and oceans, heating the surface.\n3. The warmed Earth radiates this thermal energy back into space as **longer-wavelength infrared (thermal IR) radiation**.\n4. **Greenhouse Gases (GHGs):** Gases whose asymmetrical molecular structure allows them to absorb infrared wavelengths:\n   - **Carbon Dioxide ($CO_2$):** Primary contributor ($60\\%$ contribution to enhanced effect).\n   - **Methane ($CH_4$):** 25 times more potent per molecule than $CO_2$ (from paddy fields, cattle digestion, natural gas leaks).\n   - **Nitrous Oxide ($N_2O$):** From nitrogenous fertilizers.\n   - **Water Vapour ($H_2O$):** Most abundant natural greenhouse gas.\n   - **Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs):** Synthetic refrigerants.\n5. These GHGs absorb upward-traveling IR photons and re-radiate them in all directions, including back toward the Earth's surface, trapping heat like a glass greenhouse.",
              "sciNote": "The planet Venus has an atmosphere of 96.5% CO₂, creating a runaway greenhouse effect that elevates its surface temperature to a scorching 465°C—hot enough to melt lead!",
