              "name": "Physical and Chemical Properties of Water",
              "desc": "Water possesses exceptional physical and chemical properties resulting from its bent geometry ($104.5^\\circ$ bond angle), polar $O-H$ covalent bonds, and extensive **intermolecular hydrogen bonding**:\n\n1. **High Boiling Point ($100^\\circ\\text{C}$) and Freezing Point ($0^\\circ\\text{C}$):**\nAbnormally high compared to other hydrides of group 16 ($H_2S$ boils at $-60^\\circ\\text{C}$) because significant thermal energy is required to break extensive networks of hydrogen bonds.\n\n2. **High Specific Heat Capacity ($4.184\\text{ J/g}\\cdot^\\circ\\text{C}$):**\nWater can absorb or release large quantities of heat with minimal temperature changes. This stabilizes terrestrial climates and prevents abrupt temperature swings in living organisms.\n\n3. **Anomalous Expansion and Density Maximum at 4°C:**\nUnlike nearly all other liquids that contract and become denser as they cool, water reaches its **maximum density ($1.000\\text{ g/cm}^3$) at $4^\\circ\\text{C}$**. Below $4^\\circ\\text{C}$, water molecules arrange into a rigid, open, cage-like hexagonal crystal lattice held by hydrogen bonds, creating empty space. Consequently, **ice has a lower density ($0.917\\text{ g/cm}^3$) than liquid water and floats on top**.\n*Ecological Importance:* In freezing winters, an insulating blanket of ice floats at the surface of lakes and rivers while liquid water remains at $4^\\circ\\text{C}$ beneath it, preserving aquatic life.\n\n4. **High Heat of Vaporization ($2.26\\times 10^6\\text{ J/kg}$):**\nProvides efficient evaporative cooling (e.g. human sweating and plant transpiration).\n\n5. **Universal Solvent Action:**\nWater dissolves more substances than any other liquid due to its **high polarity** and **high dielectric constant ($78.5$ at 25°C)**, which weakens electrostatic attractions between ions in crystal lattices, surrounding cations and anions with hydrating water shells (hydration).",
