              "desc": "**1. Soft Water:**\nWater that produces **rich, copious lather readily** with ordinary soap. It is free from dissolved divalent calcium and magnesium ions (e.g. distilled water, rain water).\n\n**2. Hard Water:**\nWater that **does not produce lather readily** with soap, but instead forms a sticky, insoluble curd or white precipitate called **scum**.\n\n**Chemistry of Scum Formation:**\nOrdinary soap is the sodium salt of a long-chain fatty acid, such as **sodium stearate ($C_{17}H_{35}COONa$)**. When soap is added to hard water containing dissolved calcium ($Ca^{2+}$) or magnesium ($Mg^{2+}$) ions, insoluble calcium and magnesium stearates precipitate out as scum:\n$$2C_{17}H_{35}COONa(aq) + Ca^{2+}(aq) \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text{Insoluble Scum}}{(C_{17}H_{35}COO)_2Ca \\downarrow} + 2Na^+(aq)$$\n$$2C_{17}H_{35}COONa(aq) + Mg^{2+}(aq) \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text{Insoluble Scum}}{(C_{17}H_{35}COO)_2Mg \\downarrow} + 2Na^+(aq)$$\nSoap cannot produce cleaning lather until all $Ca^{2+}$ and $Mg^{2+}$ ions are completely precipitated out, leading to tremendous soap wastage.",
