              "name": "Removal of Temporary Hardness (Boiling & Clark's Method)",
              "desc": "Temporary hardness can be eliminated by converting soluble bicarbonates into insoluble carbonates:\n\n1. **By Boiling:**\nHeating temporary hard water decomposes soluble calcium bicarbonate into insoluble calcium carbonate, which precipitates out as white scale and is removed by filtration:\n$$Ca(HCO_3)_2(aq) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} CaCO_3(s) \\downarrow + H_2O(l) + CO_2(g) \\uparrow$$\n$$Mg(HCO_3)_2(aq) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} MgCO_3(s) \\downarrow + H_2O(l) + CO_2(g) \\uparrow$$\n\n2. **By Clark's Method (Addition of Slaked Lime):**\nA calculated quantity of **slaked lime ($Ca(OH)_2$, calcium hydroxide)** is added to temporary hard water in municipal treatment tanks. Lime neutralizes the bicarbonates, precipitating insoluble calcium carbonate and magnesium hydroxide:\n$$Ca(HCO_3)_2(aq) + Ca(OH)_2(aq) \\rightarrow 2CaCO_3(s) \\downarrow + 2H_2O(l)$$\n$$Mg(HCO_3)_2(aq) + 2Ca(OH)_2(aq) \\rightarrow 2CaCO_3(s) \\downarrow + Mg(OH)_2(s) \\downarrow + 2H_2O(l)$$\nThe precipitate settles out and clear soft water is decanted. *(Caution: Adding excess lime must be avoided as excess Ca(OH)₂ itself makes water hard).*",
              "sciNote": "Clark's method is the most cost-effective municipal scale softening technique for temporary hard water because slaked lime is an inexpensive, readily available chemical.",
              "examTip": "Memorize the chemical equations: Boiling produces CaCO₃ + H₂O + CO₂; Clark's method uses Ca(OH)₂ and produces 2CaCO₃ + 2H₂O.",
              "concepts": [
                "Boiling decomposes bicarbonates to insoluble carbonates: Ca(HCO₃)₂ → CaCO₃↓ + H₂O + CO₂.",
                "Clark's method uses calculated slaked lime: Ca(HCO₃)₂ + Ca(OH)₂ → 2CaCO₃↓ + 2H₂O."
              ]
