              "num": "15.4.2",
              "name": "Eutrophication and Depletion of Dissolved Oxygen",
              "desc": "**Eutrophication:** The natural or accelerated ecological aging of a water body characterized by excessive nutrient enrichment (particularly **phosphates ($PO_4^{3-}$) and nitrates ($NO_3^-$)**) resulting in explosive algal growth.\n\n**Step-by-Step Mechanism of Eutrophication:**\n1. **Nutrient Inflow:** Agricultural runoff rich in nitrogenous/phosphatic fertilizers and municipal sewage rich in detergent phosphates enters a pond, lake, or slow-moving river.\n2. **Algal Bloom:** Algae and surface phytoplankton multiply explosively, forming a dense, opaque green scum covering the water surface (**Algal Bloom**).\n3. **Blockage of Sunlight:** The thick algal layer blocks sunlight from penetrating into deeper water, causing submerged aquatic plants to die because they can no longer photosynthesize.\n4. **Bacterial Decomposition:** When the massive algal mass dies, millions of aerobic decomposing bacteria multiply rapidly to break down the dead organic matter.\n5. **Dissolved Oxygen Depletion (Anoxia):** Aerobic bacteria consume virtually all dissolved oxygen ($DO$) in the water, causing Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) to skyrocket. Dissolved oxygen levels plunge toward zero.\n6. **Mass Extinction of Aquatic Fauna:** Fish, crustaceans, and other aquatic animals suffocate and die en masse. Anaerobic bacteria take over, producing foul-smelling toxic gases like hydrogen sulphide ($H_2S$) and methane ($CH_4$).",
              "sciNote": "Eutrophication has created over 400 oceanic 'dead zones' worldwide covering more than 245,000 square kilometers where marine life cannot survive.",
              "examTip": "Memorize the sequence: Nutrient runoff → Algal bloom → Sunlight blocked → Submerged plants die → Bacteria consume oxygen → Fish die.",
