        "lq": [
          {
            "q": "1. (a) Enlist the main sources of water. (b) How would you categorize physical properties of water? (c) Predict the products of reaction when (i) K, (ii) Cl₂, (iii) CaO, (iv) CH₃COONa, (v) C react with water.",
            "ans": "**(a) Main Sources of Water:**\n1. Ocean and sea water (97.2% - saline).\n2. Glaciers and polar ice caps (2.15% - frozen fresh).\n3. Underground water (0.62% - aquifers, wells).\n4. Surface fresh water (0.01% - lakes, rivers, streams, ponds).\n\n**(b) Categorization of Physical Properties of Water:**\n1. *Thermal Properties:* High boiling point ($100^\\circ\\text{C}$), freezing point ($0^\\circ\\text{C}$), high specific heat capacity ($4.184\\text{ J/g}\\cdot^\\circ\\text{C}$), and high heat of vaporization.\n2. *Density Properties:* Maximum density of $1.000\\text{ g/cm}^3$ at $4^\\circ\\text{C}$; anomalous expansion below $4^\\circ\\text{C}$ causing ice to float.\n3. *Solvent Properties:* Highly polar liquid with dielectric constant of $78.5$, acting as a universal solvent.\n\n**(c) Reactions with Water:**\n- **(i) Potassium ($K$):** Reacts violently forming potassium hydroxide and hydrogen gas:\n  $$2K + 2H_2O \\rightarrow 2KOH + H_2 \\uparrow$$\n- **(ii) Chlorine ($Cl_2$):** Dissolves and reacts to form hydrochloric and hypochlorous acids:\n  $$Cl_2 + H_2O \\rightarrow HCl + HOCl$$\n- **(iii) Calcium Oxide ($CaO$):** Highly exothermic slaking reaction forming calcium hydroxide:\n  $$CaO + H_2O \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2 + \\text{Heat}$$\n- **(iv) Sodium Acetate ($CH_3COONa$):** Hydrolyzes to form basic solution:\n  $$CH_3COONa + H_2O \\rightleftharpoons CH_3COOH + NaOH$$\n- **(v) Carbon / Red-hot Coke ($C$):** Reacts with steam at 1000°C to form Water Gas ($CO + H_2$):\n  $$C(s) + H_2O(g) \\xrightarrow{1000^\\circ\\text{C}} CO(g) + H_2(g)$$"
          },
          {
            "q": "2. Water is an excellent solvent. Explain how this property is beneficial for life but sometimes harmful for us.",
            "ans": "**Why Water is an Excellent Solvent:**\nDue to its bent geometry, high polarity ($O^{\\delta-}-H^{\\delta+}$), and high dielectric constant ($78.5$), water dissolves both ionic compounds (via ion-dipole hydration) and polar covalent compounds (via hydrogen bonding).\n\n**How this Property is Beneficial for Life:**\n1. **Biological Transport:** Dissolves essential nutrients, glucose, amino acids, mineral electrolytes, and hormones in blood plasma and transports them throughout body cells.\n2. **Metabolic Medium:** All biochemical enzymatic reactions in living cells occur in aqueous solution.\n3. **Plant Nutrition:** Soil water dissolves essential mineral nitrates, phosphates, and potassium ions and conducts them up plant roots via xylem.\n\n**How this Property is Harmful for Us:**\n1. **Picks Up Hardness:** As rain water seeps through rocks, it dissolves calcium and magnesium minerals, turning into hard water that wastes soap and ruins industrial boilers.\n2. **Spreads Pollution:** Dissolves toxic heavy metals ($Pb, As, Cd$), poisonous agricultural pesticides (DDT), and industrial effluents, contaminating drinking aquifers.\n3. **Carrier for Pathogens:** Acts as a vehicle for transmitting deadly water-borne bacterial and viral pathogens."
          },
          {
            "q": "3. Classify temporary hard and permanent hard water. (a) What methods could be adopted to remove temporary hardness? (b) What methods could be adopted to remove permanent hardness?",
            "ans": "**Classification of Water Hardness:**\n- **Temporary Hardness:** Caused by dissolved calcium and magnesium **bicarbonates** ($Ca(HCO_3)_2, Mg(HCO_3)_2$). Easily removed by simple boiling.\n- **Permanent Hardness:** Caused by dissolved calcium and magnesium **chlorides and sulphates** ($CaCl_2, CaSO_4, MgCl_2, MgSO_4$). Cannot be removed by boiling.\n\n**(a) Methods to Remove Temporary Hardness:**\n1. **Boiling:** Decomposes soluble bicarbonates into insoluble carbonates:\n   $$Ca(HCO_3)_2 \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} CaCO_3 \\downarrow + H_2O + CO_2 \\uparrow$$\n2. **Clark's Method (Slaked Lime):** Adding calculated calcium hydroxide:\n   $$Ca(HCO_3)_2 + Ca(OH)_2 \\rightarrow 2CaCO_3 \\downarrow + 2H_2O$$\n\n**(b) Methods to Remove Permanent Hardness:**\n1. **Washing Soda ($Na_2CO_3$ Method):** Precipitates divalent cations as insoluble carbonates:\n   $$CaSO_4 + Na_2CO_3 \\rightarrow CaCO_3 \\downarrow + Na_2SO_4$$\n   $$CaCl_2 + Na_2CO_3 \\rightarrow CaCO_3 \\downarrow + 2NaCl$$\n2. **Ion-Exchange (Sodium Zeolite Process):** Percolating hard water through a sodium zeolite bed ($Na_2Z$):\n   $$Na_2Z + Ca^{2+} \\rightarrow CaZ + 2Na^+$$"
          },
          {
            "q": "4. (a) Why is water important for us? (b) Write the disadvantages of hard water. (c) Enlist the advantages of wastewater treatment.",
            "ans": "**(a) Importance of Water:**\nWater sustains all cellular life, regulates body temperature via sweating, lubricates joints and organs, conducts nutrients in blood plasma, and is indispensable for global agriculture, sanitation, and industrial manufacturing.\n\n**(b) Disadvantages of Hard Water:**\n1. **Wastage of Soap:** Reacts with soap to form insoluble scum, consuming excess soap before lather forms.\n2. **Boiler Scale:** Deposits insulating crusts of $CaCO_3$ and $CaSO_4$ inside boilers, wasting 30–50% fuel and risking catastrophic steam explosions.\n3. **Clogs Piping:** Accumulates in hot-water pipes and taps, reducing flow.\n4. **Damages Textiles:** Forms spots on washed fabrics in industrial textile dyeing.\n\n**(c) Advantages of Wastewater Treatment:**\n1. Prevents water-borne disease outbreaks (cholera, typhoid, hepatitis).\n2. Protects aquatic fisheries and prevents river eutrophication.\n3. Enables water recycling for agricultural irrigation and industrial cooling, conserving scarce freshwater reserves."
          },
          {
            "q": "5. How would you evaluate the effects of water pollution? Explain how industrial wastes, domestic wastes, and agricultural wastes pollute the environment.",
            "ans": "**Evaluation of Water Pollution Effects:**\nWater pollution destroys aquatic ecosystems, renders water undrinkable, causes devastating epidemics, and poisons human food chains through biomagnification.\n\n1. **Industrial Wastes:** Factories discharge untreated effluents containing toxic heavy metals ($Pb, Hg, Cd, Cr$) and strong acids. Heavy metals bioaccumulate in fish and cause severe brain, kidney, and neurological damage in humans.\n2. **Domestic Wastes:** Untreated sewage introduces disease-causing pathogenic bacteria and viruses into drinking supplies. Synthetic detergents cause persistent foam that blocks dissolved oxygen and contains phosphates that trigger eutrophication.\n3. **Agricultural Wastes:** Runoff containing chemical fertilizers (nitrates and phosphates) causes rapid eutrophication and oxygen depletion, killing fish. Toxic pesticides and insecticides seep into groundwater and poison farm communities."
          }
        ]
