              "num": "16.1.2",
              "name": "Extraction of Copper from Copper Pyrite Ore",
              "desc": "Copper is commonly extracted from **copper pyrite ($CuFeS_2$ or chalcopyrite)** through four sequential pyrometallurgical steps:\n\n1. **Roasting:**\nThe concentrated ore is heated strongly in a reverberatory furnace in a continuous current of excess air below its melting point. Moisture and volatile impurities ($As, Sb, S$) escape as volatile oxides, while copper pyrite is partially converted into cuprous sulphide ($Cu_2S$) and ferrous sulphide ($FeS$):\n$$2CuFeS_2 + O_2 \\rightarrow Cu_2S + 2FeS + SO_2 \\uparrow$$\n$$2FeS + 3O_2 \\rightarrow 2FeO + 2SO_2 \\uparrow$$\n\n2. **Smelting:**\nThe roasted ore is mixed with **sand ($SiO_2$, acidic flux)** and coke and heated in a blast furnace. Ferrous oxide reacts with sand flux to form molten iron silicate **slag**, which floats on top and is skimmed off:\n$$FeO + SiO_2 \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text{Molten Slag (Iron Silicate)}}{FeSiO_3}$$\nThe heavier molten layer that collects at the bottom is called **Matte**—a molten mixture of **cuprous sulphide and residual ferrous sulphide ($Cu_2S + FeS$)** containing about $45–50\\%$ copper.\n\n3. **Bessemerization:**\nMolten matte is transferred into a pear-shaped **Bessemer Converter** lined with basic refractories. A blast of hot air and sand is injected through tuyeres:\n- Remaining $FeS$ is oxidized to $FeO$ and removed as slag ($FeSiO_3$).\n- Cuprous sulphide undergoes self-reduction:\n  $$2Cu_2S + 3O_2 \\rightarrow 2Cu_2O + 2SO_2 \\uparrow$$\n  $$2Cu_2O + Cu_2S \\rightarrow 6Cu + SO_2 \\uparrow$$\nAs the molten copper cools and solidifies, escaping $SO_2$ gas forms blisters on the metallic surface. This product is about **$98\\%$ pure** and is known as **Blister Copper**.\n\n4. **Electrolytic Refining of Blister Copper:**\nTo achieve $99.99\\%$ electrical-grade purity, blister copper is refined in an electrolytic bath:\n- **Anode:** Thick plates of impure blister copper.\n- **Cathode:** Thin sheets of 100% pure copper.\n- **Electrolyte:** Aqueous copper sulphate solution ($CuSO_4$) acidified with dilute $H_2SO_4$.\n- *Anode Reaction (Oxidation):* $Cu(\\text{impure}) \\rightarrow Cu^{2+}(aq) + 2e^-$\n- *Cathode Reaction (Reduction):* $Cu^{2+}(aq) + 2e^- \\rightarrow Cu(\\text{pure, deposits})$\nPure copper plates out smoothly on the cathode. Valuable noble metals (gold, silver, platinum) do not dissolve and settle below the anode as **anode mud / slime**.",
              "sciNote": "The recovered anode mud from copper electro-refining contains enough gold and silver to pay for the entire electrical operational cost of the refinery!",
              "examTip": "Memorize: Matte = Cu₂S + FeS; Blister copper = 98% pure copper; Anode = impure copper; Cathode = pure copper.",
