              "name": "Origin, Refining and Fractional Distillation of Petroleum",
              "desc": "**Petroleum (Crude Oil):**\nDerived from Latin *petra* (rock) and *oleum* (oil). It is a naturally occurring complex, dark brownish-black viscous liquid mixture consisting of hundreds of different hydrocarbons (chiefly alkanes, cycloalkanes, and aromatic hydrocarbons) along with minor compounds of sulphur, nitrogen, and oxygen.\n\n**Origin of Petroleum (Biogenic / Organic Theory):**\nFormed hundreds of millions of years ago by the anaerobic decay of microscopic marine organisms (plankton, algae) buried deep under subterranean sedimentary mud and silt under intense pressure and bacterial heat over geological eras.\n\n**Refining of Petroleum:**\nThe process of separating crude oil into useful commercial hydrocarbon fractions based on their different boiling points is called **refining**, carried out in a **fractional distillation column**:\n- Crude oil is desalted, preheated to about **$400^\\circ\\text{C}$** in a pipe furnace, and pumped into the base of a tall fractionating column fitted with horizontal bubble-cap trays.\n- As hot vapours rise, temperature drops gradually up the tower.\n- Heavier hydrocarbons with higher boiling points condense in lower trays; lighter hydrocarbons with lower boiling points rise higher before condensing; non-condensable gases escape from the top.",
