            "q": "10. How is blister copper purified?",
            "ans": "Blister copper ($~98\\%$ pure) is purified to $99.99\\%$ electrical purity by **Electrolytic Refining**:\n- **Anode:** Thick slabs of impure blister copper.\n- **Cathode:** Thin sheets of pure copper.\n- **Electrolyte:** Aqueous copper sulphate solution ($CuSO_4$) acidified with dilute sulphuric acid ($H_2SO_4$).\n- **Current:** Direct current causes copper atoms from the anode to dissolve as $Cu^{2+}$ ions into solution and deposit onto the cathode:\n  $$\\text{Anode: } Cu(\\text{impure}) \\rightarrow Cu^{2+} + 2e^-$$\n  $$\\text{Cathode: } Cu^{2+} + 2e^- \\rightarrow Cu(\\text{pure})$$\nImpure anode copper dissolves away, pure copper plates out on the cathode, and precious metals (gold, silver) settle as anode mud."
